ROS 2 Software Architecture for Autonomous Mobile Robot
1. List of Necessary ROS 2 Nodes and Responsibilities
•	map_server:
•	Description: Loads a pre-built static map of the environment.
•	Responsibility: Publishes the map to /map topic.
•	amcl (Adaptive Monte Carlo Localization):
•	Description: Estimates the robot's pose within the map using sensor data and motion models.
•	Responsibility: Subscribes to /scan, /odom, and /map. Publishes estimated pose to /amcl_pose.
•	global_planner:
•	Description: Generates a global path from the current pose to the goal pose.
•	Responsibility: Subscribes to /map and /move_base_simple/goal. Publishes global path to /global_plan.
•	local_planner:
•	Description: Refines the global path into a feasible trajectory considering dynamic obstacles.
•	Responsibility: Subscribes to /scan, /odom, and /global_plan. Publishes local path to /local_plan.
•	base_controller:
•	Description: Converts the local path into velocity commands for the robot's motors.
•	Responsibility: Subscribes to /local_plan and /odom. Publishes velocity commands to /cmd_vel.
•	lidar_driver:
•	Description: Interfaces with the 2D LiDAR sensor.
•	Responsibility: Publishes raw scan data to /scan.
•	odometry_node:
•	Description: Computes the robot's odometry based on wheel encoders.
•	Responsibility: Publishes odometry data to /odom.
•	goal_publisher:
•	Description: Allows user input or other nodes to specify a goal pose.
•	Responsibility: Publishes goal pose to /move_base_simple/goal.
2. Key Topics, Message Types, and Node Interactions
•	/map
•	Message Type: nav_msgs/msg/OccupancyGrid
•	Publisher: map_server
•	Subscribers: amcl, global_planner
•	/scan
•	Message Type: sensor_msgs/msg/LaserScan
•	Publisher: lidar_driver
•	Subscribers: amcl, local_planner
•	/odom
•	Message Type: nav_msgs/msg/Odometry
•	Publisher: odometry_node
•	Subscribers: amcl, local_planner, base_controller
•	/amcl_pose
•	Message Type: geometry_msgs/msg/PoseWithCovarianceStamped
•	Publisher: amcl
•	Subscribers: Not used directly in this architecture, but can be used for debugging and visualization.
•	/global_plan
•	Message Type: nav_msgs/msg/Path
•	Publisher: global_planner
•	Subscribers: local_planner
•	/local_plan
•	Message Type: nav_msgs/msg/Path
•	Publisher: local_planner
•	Subscribers: base_controller
•	/cmd_vel
•	Message Type: geometry_msgs/msg/Twist
•	Publisher: base_controller
•	Subscribers: Robot's motor controllers (not explicitly modeled in this architecture).
•	/move_base_simple/goal
•	Message Type: geometry_msgs/msg/PoseStamped
•	Publisher: goal_publisher
•	Subscribers: global_planner
3. Data Flow Explanation
1.	Initialization:
•	map_server loads the map and publishes it to /map.
•	goal_publisher receives a goal pose from the user and publishes it to /move_base_simple/goal.
2.	Localization:
•	lidar_driver continuously scans the environment and publishes the data to /scan.
•	odometry_node computes and publishes the robot's odometry to /odom.
•	amcl subscribes to /scan, /odom, and /map to estimate the robot's pose and publishes the estimated pose to /amcl_pose.
3.	Global Path Planning:
•	global_planner subscribes to /map and /move_base_simple/goal to compute a global path and publishes it to /global_plan.
4.	Local Path Planning:
•	local_planner subscribes to /scan, /odom, and /global_plan to refine the global path into a local trajectory that avoids obstacles and publishes it to /local_plan.
5.	Control:
•	base_controller subscribes to /local_plan and /odom to generate velocity commands for the robot and publishes them to /cmd_vel.
This architecture ensures that the robot navigates efficiently from its current location to the specified goal while avoiding obstacles dynamically.

